In this lesson we'll look at how to classify triangles, first by their sides and then by their angles, and finally see how you can use both labels together. Let's start with the side-length family. If all three sides are the same length, it's called equilateral, and every angle inside is sixty degrees. If at least two sides match, it's isosceles, and the two angles opposite those equal sides, called the base angles, are equal too. If all three sides are different lengths, it's scalene, and all three angles are different as well. Now, sorting by angle. When every angle is less than ninety degrees, the triangle is acute. When one angle is exactly ninety, it's a right triangle. And when one angle is bigger than ninety, it's obtuse, which means the other two angles have to be acute, because all three interior angles always add up to one hundred and eighty degrees. You can combine both labels, like a right-angled isosceles triangle, which has one ninety-degree angle and two equal forty-five-degree angles. Now let's see it in action.